{"name":"Clover Mist","hex":"#6fc288","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clover","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Trifolium with leaves usually divided into three (rarely four) leaflets and with white or red flowers.","The second Lenormand card, representing hope, optimism and short-term luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clover"},{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clover","blocks":[{"text":"Clovers, also called trefoils, are plants of the genus Trifolium. They are herbaceous plants growing up to 30 cm (12 in) tall. The leaves are usually trifoliate and the small flowers are reddish to white or yellow. Related genera with similar common names include Melilotus and Medicago (burclover)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/79_Trifolium_repens_L.jpg/330px-79_Trifolium_repens_L.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clover","wikidata":"Q101538"},{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist","wikidata":"Q192196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg/250px-Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg/500px-Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg/250px-Trifolium_dubium_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrifolium_dubium_RF.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg/330px-Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg/960px-Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg/330px-Trifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrifolium_alpestre_RF.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Clover_leaf_top.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Clover_leaf_top.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Clover_leaf_top.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Clover_leaf_top.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Top of Trif